 [17592186221468 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221468 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221468 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221468 #edb/ref 1045 "Fundamental Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221468 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186222332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221468 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221468 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221468 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "bf2387fe-ebed-5568-a260-0d681cb9fd28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221985 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221985 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221985 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221985 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221985 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f190e3a4-5efc-5caa-a01f-c8d68875b312" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221985 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221989 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333713" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221989 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221989 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221989 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ca7cf8a-29b9-5dc9-9eb7-2eb98441be57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221989 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+2\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221990 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221990 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line {{field-3}} by the properties of invertible matrices." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "920ced52-32ff-52a3-a836-fb31a23c82e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221990 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Recall that a matrix has linearly independent columns if and only if its determinant is nonzero. Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for any particular $t,$\n\n$$\ndet(Φ(t))\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the properties of determinants}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the properties of invertible matrices." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222332 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222332 #edb/ref 1061 594316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222332 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3b0bd285-b383-5ebb-821f-4f74a0b37719" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222332 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222333 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222333 #edb/ref 1061 594317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222333 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aa3c7790-3ab9-5519-bad3-4f82fc8ac113" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222333 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222334 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222334 #edb/ref 1061 594320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222334 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2fe7252e-7fc7-59bb-bc32-74c452b7cf73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222334 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222335 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222335 #edb/ref 1061 594321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222335 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b90cd12-9695-5b6b-b407-6eed6ae7fc84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222335 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222336 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222336 #edb/ref 1061 594325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222336 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d44c84d5-4215-5318-bba2-81eb3a34ee7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222336 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222337 #edb/ref 1061 594326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222337 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32e0e465-9256-5f88-b771-2edff24ecd54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222337 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222502 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the homogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nA **fundamental matrix** for this system is an $n\\times n$ matrix $\\Phi(t)$ whose columns are $n$ linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIf $\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\dots,\\mathbf{x}_n(t)$ are linearly independent solutions, then one fundamental matrix is\n\n$$\n\\Phi(t)=\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\dots,\\mathbf{x}_n(t)\\big].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi(t)$ can be written in any order.\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222502 #edb/ref 1065 14508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222502 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a7207a24-194d-59d6-b25c-f9bb4f1b0d6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222502 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222503 #edb/ref 1064 "Again, let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for the linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix. The fundamental matrices have the following important properties.\n\n1\\. *Satisfies the underlying system*. Indeed, since each column of $\\Phi(t)$ is a solution of the system, we have\n\n  $$\n  \\Phi'(t) = A\\,\\Phi(t).\n  $$\n\n2\\. *Invertibility*. The columns of $\\Phi(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t,$ so\n\n  $$\n  \\det\\Phi(t)\\neq 0\n  $$\n\n  for all $t.$ In particular, $\\Phi(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$\n\n3\\. *Representation of solutions*. Every solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ of the system can be written in the form\n\n  $$\n  \\mathbf{x}(t)=\\Phi(t)\\,\\mathbf{c},\n  $$\n\n  where $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\n4\\. *Non-uniqueness*. If $C$ is any invertible constant matrix, then\n\n  $$\n  \\Psi(t)=\\Phi(t)\\,C\n  $$\n\n  is also a fundamental matrix for the same system.\n\nWe'll now get some practice." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222503 #edb/ref 1065 14509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222503 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e2fdbc29-4542-5fdc-adbd-316c5dcb6878" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222503 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of the Fundamental Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222504 #edb/ref 1064 "For a linear system $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with constant $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **standard fundamental matrix** is the unique fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ that satisfies the initial condition\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(0)=I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor constant matrices $A,$ the standard fundamental matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t)=e^{At}.\n$$\n\nThis matrix satisfies\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t)=A\\,\\Phi_0(t)\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\Phi_0(0)=I_n.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Any other fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for the same system can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\Phi(t)=\\Phi_0(t)\\,C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an invertible constant matrix.\n\nIn the next slide, we will compute the standard fundamental matrix for a specific example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222504 #edb/ref 1065 14510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222504 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4f75576c-be32-5784-9fee-e1288445728c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222504 #edb/ref 1067 "Fundamental Matrices and Matrix Exponentials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305212 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{cos}(2t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305212 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305212 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06b62f64-a72a-58c3-83b7-f04a2c64a243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305540 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\operatorname{sin}(2t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305540 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 1759218604