db/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311288 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186309737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311288 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab49378a-bc07-5ec8-aa5b-7fade32fbd2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311687 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311687 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311687 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311687 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311687 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311687 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A\\,Φ(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $[a,\\,b]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $a$ and $b.$*\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix and line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311687 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72f5b665-78cb-51a0-bf20-214a07f61466" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311687 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ Line $\\text{L1}$ identifies ${x}_{2}(t)$ as a column of $Φ(t),$ so ${x}_{2}(t)$ must satisfy the differential equation. In particular,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix and line $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)],$ the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)].\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{differentiating each column}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{factoring out }A\\text{ on the left}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365668 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365668 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365668 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365668 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365668 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">${e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">${e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365668 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "162963ac-9899-549d-9b3f-b09605170db6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365668 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-5t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5t,\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-5t)={e}^{-5t} \\\\ r(5t)={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-5t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-5t} \\\\ 5t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-10t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-5t}-{e}^{5t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10t}({e}^{-5t}-{e}^{5t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-5t}+{e}^{5t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-5t}+{e}^{5t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-5t}+{e}^{5t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{5t}+{e}^{-5t}) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{10t}({e}^{-5t}-{e}^{5t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t}+{e}^{-5t} & {e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t} \\\\ {e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t} & {e}^{5t}+{e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365669 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365669 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365669 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365669 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e0c533ef-5493-5cd1-a424-830212f48ffc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365670 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{5t}+{e}^{-5t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365670 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365670 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9cd42562-09cd-51eb-b4a2-b584bedeb569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365671 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365671 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365671 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365671 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365671 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365671 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "df0b6879-e091-50fa-802c-bc06c1af37d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365672 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{5t}+{e}^{-5t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365672 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 175