tion. In particular, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L2}$ follows from the fact that ${x}_{1}\\text{ is a solution of the system}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ By definition, the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column,\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}^{′}(t) & {x}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$ from $\\text{L3},$ we substitute each derivative into the matrix to get\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substitution from $\\text{L2}\\text{ and }\\text{L3}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}]=A[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}]=AΦ(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{factoring out }A\\text{ on the left}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391265 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391265 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391265 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391265 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391265 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391265 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391265 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186391266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391265 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "85305209-f127-50f4-8fb6-558c6f7e40e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391266 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391266 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592